<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n49393" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">tŭmĕ-făcĭo</span></b> ,
			fēci, factum, 3, v. a.
		tumeo, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>to cause to swell</i>, <i>to tumefy</i> (<b>poet.</b>). </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<b>Lit.</b>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">vis fera ventorum ... Extentam tumefecit humum,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Ov.
					M. 15, 303</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">tumefactus pontus,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">id.
					ib. 11, 518</span></i>
			.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<b>Trop.</b>, <i>to swell</i> or <i>puff up</i>, <i>to inflate</i> with
			pride, etc.: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">num me laetitiā tumefactum fallis
					inani?</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Prop. 3,
					6</span></i>
			 (4, 5), 3: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">ut nostris tumefacta superbiat Umbria libris,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">id.
				4</span></i>
			 (5), 1, 63: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">dum nimium vano tumefactus nomine gaudes,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Mart.
					4, 11, 1</span></i>
			.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
